// S~S  SimpleSal signal starts  S~S

// [Command Arrays and Zones.note]      search in ssUI source files for a reference to this file.
//
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   ssUI supports a set of stored UI CMDs with parameters for ssUI and App testing/demo purposes.
   These configuration options control the integration of App and/or ssUI CMDs (in ssUI form) into
   ssUI. "in ssUI form" means "exact format for direct inclusion into the source file for ssUI".
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   #define  SSUI_OPTIN_INCLUDE_CMDS_IN_SSUI_APP
//
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The purpose of defining a specific type for "command arrays" (arrays of pointers to characters;
   the characters, when considered by the ssUI, turn out to be an ssUI command with parameters) is:
   the "Cmd_t" typedef creates a data type definition different from what looks like an AsciiA type.
   This is an attempt to place large arrays of strings and pointers to strings outside RAM memory.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Commands in a single array are a CmdArray, pointed to by a CmdArrayLink (name pointer and max_i).
   max_i for a CmdArray is the number of elements, where each element in the array is a pointer.
   The pointer to the start of the CmdArray
   Each CmdArray and its associated CmdArrayLink are includable/excludable from the build and help.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   typedef     const char    *pCmd_t;               pointer to an instance in an array of characters
//
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The user may define a series of ssUI "CMD" arrays and have that available, independent of ssUI.
   SSUI_OPTIN_INCLUDE_CMDS_IN_SSUI_APP asks ssUI to include part of the App in a ssUI data structure;
   not what is included but whether or not any CMDs have been defined to be included from the App.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The ssUI CMDs facility doesn't know or care about options at the App level; it just includes.
   SSUI_PLEASE_INCLUDE_THESE_APP_CMDS is the reference by SSUI whether any App cmds are defined or
   not; this occurs here when ssUI build an array of pointers to the cmds arrays that are defined.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Because this the rare instance where the App becomes part of ssUI, some restrictions are applied.
   When no App cmds are defined and included, SSUI_PLEASE_INCLUDE_THESE_APP_CMDS resolves to nothing.
   The concept now has a defined value to the compiler, and that value is "there is nothing here".
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   #ifndef SSUI_OPTIN_INCLUDE_CMDS_IN_SSUI_APP
//
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   This is the name of an array of pointers to Ascii character arrays which contain ssUI "CMDs",
   most ssUI CMD sequences can be defined and built in ahead of time or typed into ssUI live.
   A "CMD" is recognized by ssUI when the input parser finds a match in a table of CMDs, AND
   THEN the input FSM runs the command handler for that input, as provided by ssUI or the App.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   This is an array of pointers to Ascii arrays, each Ascii Array is Ascii-NUL terminated.  The
   array of pointers is incorporated by ssUI as one of the "cmds run <cmds>" choices.  ssUI does
   a limit check using the array's size (Compute_pCharNArray_CT below).  Each quoted Ascii-NUL
   array has an address, each address is of type "pCmd_t", and each is in the "pcCmds..." array.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   A confusing and odd effect occurs when the ',' is missing between two quoted Ascii-NUL arrays:
   the ssHL-language compiler helpfully contentates two command lines into one command line and moves on.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   #ifdef SSUI_OPTIN_INCLUDE_CMDS_IN_SSUI_APP
//
//
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The purpose of defining a specific type for "command arrays" (arrays of pointers to characters;
   the characters, when considered by the ssUI, turn out to be an ssUI command with parameters) is:
   the "Cmd_t" typedef creates a data type definition different from what looks like an AsciiA type.
   This is an attempt to place large arrays of strings and pointers to strings outside RAM memory.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Commands in a single array are a CmdArray, pointed to by a CmdArrayLink (name pointer and max_i).
   max_i for a CmdArray is the number of elements, where each element in the array is a pointer.
   The pointer to the start of the CmdArray
   Each CmdArray and its associated CmdArrayLink are includable/excludable from the build and help.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   typedef     const char    *pCmd_t;               pointer to an instance in an array of characters

// S~S  SimpleSal signal ends  S~S
